/**
@mainpage Yuzen source reference

Yuzen solves the long-wave (lubrication) equation for the height of a thin
liquid film on a substrate, coupled to a transport equation for the solute
concentration.

@warning **This is the reference for the code, not for the solver.** What the
equations are, how they are discretized, what has been verified and what has
not, and how to run a case, are all in the manual under @c docs/manual/. Read
that first. This reference answers the next question: which class does what,
and where to look before changing something.

@section mainpage_layout The shape of the tree

@c yuzen/src/ divides along one line: what is general numerics, and what is
this particular physics.

| Directory | What lives there |
|---|---|
| @c numerics/ | Linear algebra and time integration, with no knowledge of films. The CRS matrix, BiCGSTAB and its three interchangeable backends, the semi-implicit and Newton--Kantorovich time integrators. |
| @c grid/ | The staggered grid, the eight site types, and the ghost layers. |
| @c model/ | The physics. @c well_mixed/ treats the concentration as uniform across the thickness; @c ConcZ/ resolves it. |
| @c IO/ | Input parsing (TOML, and the legacy format), output writers, probes and field aggregates. |
| @c commonFunctions/ | Shape generation and small shared utilities. |

@section mainpage_models The models

Four combinations of concentration model and dimension are implemented, and
the factory MakeSolverModel() maps the two input keys onto them. All four
satisfy the ISolverModel interface, which is all the top-level driver knows.

| @c Physics.ConcField.Model | @c Grid.Dimension | Class |
|---|---|---|
| @c "WM" | @c "xh" | WellMixed_Model_1D |
| @c "WM" | @c "xyh" | WellMixed_Model_2D |
| @c "CZ" | @c "xh" | ConcZ_Model_2D (1-D film) |
| @c "CZ" | @c "xyh" | ConcZ_Model_2D (2-D film, pseudo 3-D) |

The last two are the same class: the film's dimension is decided by the grid,
and with @c ny()==1 no @f$y@f$ term is formed at all.

@section mainpage_invariants Three things that are easy to break

These are properties the code holds deliberately, and each has a test that
fails if it stops holding. They are worth knowing before changing anything
in the numerics.

@li **Results are bit-identical regardless of the OpenMP thread count.** That
    rests on fixed-block reductions in Vector, on a fixed insertion order in
    CRS_Matrix, and on the order in which the physics terms register
    themselves. Changing any of those changes the last digits.

@li **The mobility at a face must vanish when either side dries out.** It is
    the positivity preserving form of Diez and Kondic (2002), and rewriting it
    as an arithmetic mean produces negative film thickness near a contact
    line. See CZ_HeightEquation_2D::FaceMobility() and
    @c tests/test_positivity_mobility.cpp.

@li **Both height equations are conservative.** Volume is conserved exactly in
    the absence of evaporation and outflow, which the comparison in
    @c tools/concz_vs_wm.py checks alongside the agreement between the two
    models.

@section mainpage_tests Where the claims are checked

@c yuzen/tests/ holds the unit tests; @c ctest runs them along with two
solver-level comparisons that run the binary and compare the two concentration
models against each other in the limit where they should agree. The manual's
verification chapter says which claims are backed by which test, and its
companion chapter is explicit about what is *not* verified.

